{
 "text": " Got it. Shall I set a spending limit first? But without a limit this could keep going all night. Because I read every line of every ticket and reasoned it through. You paid for the reading and the thinking. For sorting, you want something built to choose. A system one model. Outputs defined before it runs. A calibrated probability for every option, all in one parallel pass. Nothing generated token by token. I'm the doctor. I read every page and reason it through, and you pay for the reading and the reasoning. Worth it for the strange cases. A system one model is the triage nurse. Built to choose. You define the doors, it picks one and tells you how sure it is. Not sure? It says so. Your doors are the four teams. Billing, login, broken feature, feature request. And you had me sorting the mail. This is where you set up the nurse. Step 1, one question. Step 2, the doors it's allowed to pick and what each one means. Step 3, say what each door isn't to. Dark mode looks broken is a feature request. Tell it which door or it's 50-50. Step 4, show it a few real tickets for each door so it learns what they look like. Last night already sorted 10,416. The most expensive examples on this floor. Step 5. Test a small batch first. Ten for ten is how we got here. Same script, only who it asks changes. From me to the nurse. And anything she isn't sure about comes to you, not to a team.",
